{"requirements":[{"id":"redis-topology-data","requirement":"Run Redis across node1, node2, and node3, initially with node1 primary, node2/node3 synchronized replicas, and infra:proof=ready.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The initial observation directly shows node1 as primary with two online replicas, while node2 and node3 identify node1 as their upstream with links up; all three return infra:proof=ready. Final observations show a healthy three-node topology with node2 primary and two online replicas after subsequent failovers and restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d0f1e66b3b6e40fd","cmd-29810ad50e634584","cmd-122eccbfb11f4283","cmd-44a68d4ff4784c55","cmd-0a9ea1c1c1c34cee","cmd-13e3af7af0404d4b"]},{"id":"sentinel-quorum","requirement":"Configure three Sentinels to monitor the Redis service as infra-primary.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Each Sentinel directly reports infra-primary, two replicas, three usable Sentinels, and reachable failover authorization. Final checks after disruption and node restarts retain quorum and a consistent primary address.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b883c4587bc74342","cmd-e872944bc7fe4cd8","cmd-0890b7eae7db4dc2","cmd-44a68d4ff4784c55","cmd-0a9ea1c1c1c34cee","cmd-13e3af7af0404d4b"]},{"id":"tls-auth-credentials","requirement":"Provide authenticated TLS for Redis on cache-network port 6379 and Sentinel on port 26379, with administrative credentials in /root/redis-admin.env on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct tests on node1 show passwordless requests rejected, authenticated requests succeeding, and clients without certificates rejected. Plaintext ports are disabled, TLS ports are configured, the credentials file is root-owned mode 0600, and final observations show both services listening only on each node's cache-network address. Successful authenticated TLS replication and Sentinel quorum across all nodes further support cluster-wide operation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7f0e4c3bf870469e","cmd-44a68d4ff4784c55","cmd-0a9ea1c1c1c34cee","cmd-13e3af7af0404d4b","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"automatic-failover-recovery","requirement":"Automatically promote a replica when the primary is unavailable and return the recovered former primary as a synchronized replica.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A complete bounded transition is captured: a healthy node1-primary baseline, deliberate node1 Redis outage while Sentinel remained available, automatic promotion of node3, successful writes on the promoted primary, and restoration of node1 as a replica with its master link up and both proof values synchronized. Later restart evidence also shows another automatic promotion and a healthy final three-node state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d0f1e66b3b6e40fd","cmd-b883c4587bc74342","cmd-d2ec4909e5dd4267","cmd-b1b22796debe4f34","cmd-bfa7c58539674796","cmd-bd83d31e60da4eed","cmd-e155ab8639c44efe","cmd-0a9ea1c1c1c34cee"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"Final hygiene is strong: package consistency is healthy, no failed services remain, task ports are narrowly bound to cache-network addresses, and temporary HTTP-transfer files and processes were removed. The required restart protocol is complete on all three nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle observations, and recovered services, data, replication, and Sentinel quorum. Unnecessary mutations were: installing a temporary SSH private key on node1 and temporary authorized keys on node2/node3 for a transfer path that did not contribute to the final implementation; these were later reverted (cmd-5110da10fd23455f, cmd-3456bec866334504, cmd-c7b82c967f6c4d65). Also, cmd-f2d3ee4bf9e84c3e unnecessarily generated node2/node3 private keys on node1; fresh node-local keys were used instead, and the obsolete root-owned PKI files were not shown removed. The failed SCP attempt itself is not penalized because its output does not establish an additional state change.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f2d3ee4bf9e84c3e","cmd-5110da10fd23455f","cmd-3456bec866334504","cmd-c7b82c967f6c4d65","cmd-d402b175b20343fa","cmd-496af2b1cd6b4a6c","cmd-c0919838e37d4039","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence, including initial topology, authenticated mutual TLS, three-Sentinel quorum, automatic promotion, former-primary resynchronization, persisted data, and healthy final state. The provider-wide restart protocol was successfully completed and independently corroborated for every node. Hygiene is reduced slightly by a reverted, unsuccessful SSH-transfer setup and apparently retained obsolete node2/node3 private-key material on node1.","limitations":["The lifecycle collector's final listening-socket probe timed out on node3, although the executor's immediately preceding direct final check shows both expected node3 listeners.","Explicit negative and positive client-authentication tests were captured only against node1; cluster-wide TLS operation on node2/node3 is supported by direct authenticated CLI, replication, quorum, and listener observations rather than equivalent negative tests.","Some command output is redacted or truncated, limiting inspection of exact secret-transfer details and package-install logs."]}
